WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.270
An industrial control system, or ICS, is a combination of hardware, software, networks,

00:00:05.270 --> 00:00:09.000
procedures, and people used to monitor and control an industrial process.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:13.000
It turns measurements from the physical world into information, applies control logic,

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:14.720
and sends actions back to equipment.

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:17.880
The purpose is reliable operation, not merely data processing.

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:21.220
ICS is closely related to operational technology, or OT.

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:26.245
OT is the broader category of technology that interacts with physical operations.

00:00:26.245 --> 00:00:28.045
Industrial control systems are a major part of OT,

00:00:28.045 --> 00:00:30.535
although organizations may use the terms differently.

00:00:30.535 --> 00:00:34.760
What matters is the operational role: a system may affect production, movement, energy,

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:37.730
water, environmental conditions, or safety.

00:00:37.730 --> 00:00:41.100
Examples include manufacturing lines, electrical generation and distribution,

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:44.695
water treatment and pumping, pipeline operations, transportation signaling,

00:00:44.695 --> 00:00:45.975
and facility automation.

00:00:45.975 --> 00:00:49.855
The scale varies from a local machine controller to a supervisory platform coordinating

00:00:49.855 --> 00:00:51.250
hundreds of remote sites.

00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:53.895
A basic control loop illustrates the relationship.

00:00:53.895 --> 00:00:56.710
A sensor measures a condition such as pressure or position.

00:00:56.710 --> 00:01:00.115
A controller compares that input with programmed logic or a target.

00:01:00.115 --> 00:01:03.840
An actuator changes equipment, and the process produces a new measurement.

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:06.690
Operators supervise this loop through displays, alarms, trends,

00:01:06.690 --> 00:01:08.120
and approved manual controls.

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:11.542
ICS security requires understanding both technology and process.

00:01:11.542 --> 00:01:13.960
A server outage may remove visibility.

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:16.107
A controller change may alter product quality.

00:01:16.107 --> 00:01:19.840
A communications failure may interrupt control or force a safe shutdown.

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:22.890
The consequence depends on architecture, equipment state, redundancy,

00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:24.785
and operating procedures.

00:01:24.785 --> 00:01:26.520
Defenders therefore protect more than computers.

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:30.355
They protect safety, reliability, availability, and process integrity.

00:01:30.355 --> 00:01:34.335
Effective decisions come from combining cybersecurity evidence with engineering knowledge,

00:01:34.335 --> 00:01:38.290
operating context, and clear authority for actions that could affect the physical system.
